        ["A Quant section allows 26 minutes for 15 questions. What is the average time available per question?", ["About 1 minute 44 seconds", "About 2 minutes 15 seconds", "About 1 minute 15 seconds", "About 2 minutes 30 seconds"], "26 ÷ 15 = 1.73 minutes, and 0.73 × 60 ≈ 44 seconds. Always confirm current section timing on ets.org."],
        ["What is the BEST use of the on-screen calculator in the Quant measure?", ["Confirming tedious arithmetic you cannot simplify", "Checking every answer before moving to the next item", "Replacing estimation on answer choices that are far apart", "Converting all fractions to decimals before comparing"], "The calculator is slow. Estimation and number sense are faster when choices are spread out; reserve the calculator for unavoidable messy computation."],
        ["A learner's diagnostic test shows Verbal 141 and Quant 147. After building an error log, what should she do NEXT?", ["Group misses by topic and cause to target the biggest patterns", "Retake full practice tests daily until the score rises", "Study every GRE topic equally from the start of the book", "Focus only on Quant, since it is already the higher score"], "Error patterns (concept gap, careless, timing) show where points are cheapest to gain. Daily full tests without analysis and equal coverage of all topics waste limited study time."],
        ["Which statement about moving between sections on the GRE is accurate?", ["You can revisit questions within a section but not earlier sections", "You can return to any section until the whole test ends", "You cannot change an answer once you move to the next item", "You may revisit earlier sections if time remains at the end"], "Mark and review works only inside the current section. Once a section is closed, it is final."],
        ["Why are official ETS practice tests preferred over most third-party tests for score prediction?", ["They best reflect the real question style and scoring", "They are always harder, which builds a safety margin", "They contain the exact questions used on test day", "They include extra sections that are no longer used"], "Official materials are built by the test writer. Third-party tests vary in difficulty and style, and no practice test reproduces live test items."],
        ["A working adult has 8 weeks to prepare. Which study structure is MOST likely to produce durable gains?", ["Short, frequent mixed-topic sessions with timed tests every 1-2 weeks", "One long weekend session per week focused on a single topic", "Reading strategy books for 6 weeks, then practice questions in the last 2", "Daily full-length practice tests with no review of missed questions"], "Spaced, interleaved practice with regular feedback from timed tests improves retention and transfer, the same principle used in motor learning."],
        ["What does a percentile rank of 60 next to a Verbal score indicate?", ["60% of recent test takers scored below that score", "The test taker answered 60% of Verbal questions correctly", "The score is 60 points above the lowest possible score", "The test taker is in the top 60% of all test takers"], "Percentile compares you with other test takers. It is not a percent-correct value; 'top 60%' misreads the direction."],
        ["A student's first GRE attempt was weaker than hoped. Which ETS policy MOST directly limits the impact of that attempt on applications?", ["Choosing which test dates' scores to send", "Automatic averaging of all attempts", "Deleting the lowest section after the fact", "Scores expiring after one year"], "ScoreSelect lets you send chosen test dates. ETS does not average attempts, and scores are reportable for five years, not one."]
